ptsy into a symbol of radical religious reform."}], "places": [{"name": "St. Petersburg, Russia", "role": "Major center"}, {"name": "Moscow, Russia", "role": "Significant hub"}, {"name": "Kazan, Russia", "role": "Influential region"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Skoptsy", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "skoptsy"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q549628", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skoptsy"], "tags": ["Christian denomination", "Religious Institutions", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Skoptsy contributed to the historical record of the Early Modern period. Key lasting impacts include: Shaped institutional landscape and community development. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "The Skoptsy movement significantly shaped the discourse on religious dissent in Russia, influencing policies on sectarianism and bodily autonomy that would resonate through the 19th century and beyond.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-02T20:58:35Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#28620725752", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Russian christian movement which practiced castration and mastectomy Founded 1765.", "newValue": "The Skoptsy, an esoteric Christian sect originating in Russia in the 18th century, emerged as a radical reaction to the prevailing orthodox Christianity and societal norms of the time. Founded by the mystic and self-proclaimed prophet, Ivan Ivanovich, in 1765, this movement is notorious for its extreme practices, including voluntary castration and mastectomy, which its adherents believed were essential for achieving spiritual purity and embodying the perfect Christian life. Operating primarily in Russia but with reach into parts of Europe, the Skoptsy represented a significant cultural and religious phenomenon during the Early Modern period, reflecting broader themes of religious dissent and radicalism.

